<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phasis (town)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phasis was an ancient Greek colony on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, founded by settlers from Miletus in the 6th century BCE near the mouth of the Phasis river (Rioni), close to modern Poti in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. It was the main port of Colchis, linking the Greek world with the river route into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and later a Roman and Byzantine fortress.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phasis-town.jpg" alt="Phasis (town)" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Butler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colchis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Phasis lay on the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> at the mouth of the river of the same name in Colchis; the modern port city of Poti may stand on or near its site. The name is first recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s Theogony, around 700 BCE, for the river, and the town took its name from it. Its origin is disputed: since Erich Diehl suggested a non-Greek source in 1938, scholars have proposed a local river name, Proto-Georgian-Zan or Svan forms, and even a Semitic word meaning a gold river. The word pheasant comes from the name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Heraclides, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela">Pomponius Mela</a> and Stephanus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> report that Milesians founded it. The Periplus of Pseudo-Scylax calls it a Greek polis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> a trading place, and its constitution was among the 158 of the Aristotelian corpus. It was probably a mixed city in which Greek settlers lived peacefully with the local people, and it seems to have been part of the trade route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> attested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Pliny.</p><p>Phasis came under Roman control in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a>; there, in 65 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> met Servilius, the admiral of his Euxine fleet. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazic-war">Lazic War</a> between the Eastern Roman and Sassanid empires (542–562) the Persian army besieged it without success. After the coming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> it was the seat of a Greek diocese, one of whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, Cyrus, became Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> between 630 and 641.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The exact site has not been identified. The French traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-chardin">Jean Chardin</a> looked for it in the 1670s, and the Swiss scholar Frédéric Dubois de Montpéreux, who visited between 1831 and 1834, concluded that the town lay east of Poti and stood at different places over time as the land changed. The ruins of Najikhuri, long identified with a fortress described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, were demolished for an airfield in 1959 and 1960. Surface and underwater research suggests that the lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes beside the town has engulfed all or part of it; a nearby lake mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathias">Agathias</a> is identified with Lake Paliastomi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phasis (town)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
